<Layout
  title={'Structure First: Planetary Forecasting as a Skeleton Plus a Residual'}
  description={'About: Structure First. Forecast planetary climate from a small structural skeleton and study the residual. Two preregistered hindcasts on Earth temperature; protocols for Mars, Mercury and a laboratory scaling ladder.'}
>
  <section class="px-6 md:px-10 pt-16 md:pt-24 pb-20 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <p class="section-label mb-4">Research topic · work in progress · about</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-brand-white tracking-tight">
      Structure First: Planetary Forecasting as a Skeleton Plus a Residual
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 font-body text-base text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
      Most atmospheric models learn correlations from the whole record. This topic forecasts from a few structural drivers first and treats what they leave unexplained, the residual, as the object of study. Two preregistered hindcasts test the idea on four records of Earth's monthly global temperature from 1990 to 2026, a third tests it on Viking and InSight surface pressure on Mars, and a fourth on MESSENGER's record of Mercury's sodium exosphere. The same protocol is laid out for Mars, Mercury and laboratory rotating-flow experiments.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-8 glass-card p-6">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-[2px] uppercase text-brand-gray mb-3">In plain words</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 font-body text-sm text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
        <li>Start from the one or two causes known to drive the climate, and forecast only what they leave unexplained.</li>
        <li>On Earth's global temperature, a straight line in carbon dioxide forecasts two to ten years ahead with 32 to 73% less mean square error than a pattern-fitting model with twelve times as many settings.</li>
        <li>What the line misses is mostly El Niño. Adding it helps for a few months; beyond a year, nothing tried here beats the line alone.</li>
        <li>On Mars, after the seasons are removed, something slow, probably dust, is still left in the pressure record. Finding it is the next step.</li>
        <li>On Mercury, last year's sodium at the same point in the orbit predicts most of this year's. What it misses sits near the poles, where the magnetosphere delivers solar-wind ions to the surface.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
